On 2007-06-10, Henry J Cobb <hcobb@io.com> wrote:
> Given that Chaos lacks decent weapons and MR, here's my take.

Many of us would consider this a balance feature; specifically, chaotics
get a bunch of advantages (easy murder, less mysterious force, you get to
be elves, &c.) and preventing them from wishing for free MR helps offset
some of those advantages. Why do you think this needs to be changed?

> This would be the first sac gift for Rogues.
>
> Shadow Blade (chaotic silver dagger)

In and of itself this is not a bad idea in my view; more first sacrifice
gifts could help differentiate classes and gods (which is also being
discussed in another thread). An artifact silver dagger seems a little
munchkin, but you can't wield it in your offhand, so you'll probably want
something better in the late game.  OK. 

> Wielded
> Confers magic resistance and infravision.

What? Having your first sac gift give MR is a _very_ significant
balance change. This changes the method of playing an early rogue very
significantly.  Now all of the sudden finding an altar really breaks
open your game in a way that Grimtooth or even the totally wield-worthy
Stormbringer doesn't. The only class that currently gets an MR artifact as
a first sac gift is wizard, and that would be broken too if they didn't
already have MR from their cloaks. (Whether or not that is broken I'm
not even going to touch.) 

You may have some grand plan for this MR, but it just comes off as "hey
I'll post a YANI that will make the game way easier for my preferred
class, wouldn't that be awesome?" While you're at least a level above
donut-shaped boulder golems, I don't think you're really convincing
anyone to implement or play your patches here.

And infravision --- while actually a neat thing to give with a weapon ---
will only help Human rogues. Why do orcs care?

> +d20 damage against creatures of light (y and A)

Cute? Yes. Useful? Not really. If you really want to give rogues a hand
up in the early game, give them +5 to hit and a couple extra points
of damage. This is more a flavor thing than anything else.

> If the wielder is in an unlit square then it confers displacement as well.
>
> Invoked
> Casts darkness as cursed or confused scroll of light.

That combination is actually rather nice. I like that it prevents the
player from using a light source in order to use the ability, which means
no boots of jumping and limited visibility. However I'm unconvinced it
would be used enough or have enough affect to be worth implementing. It
would take some playtesting, I guess.

